## Uniqueness Guaranteed

A **Set** is like a Vec that automatically prevents duplicates. Every item is either in the Set or not — there's no "how many times."

### Creating and Using a Set

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let tags: Set[Str] = Set[Str].new();

  tags.insert("sunset");
  tags.insert("beach");
  tags.insert("friends");
  tags.insert("sunset");  // Duplicate — silently ignored!

  io.println(tags.contains(&"beach"));    // true
  io.println(tags.contains(&"mountain"));  // false

  tags.remove(&"beach");
  io.println(tags.contains(&"beach"));    // false

  return 0;
}
```

### Building a Tag Manager

```xiom
type Photo = {
  id: Int;
  tags: Set[Str];
}

fn add_tag(photo: &mut Photo, tag: Str) {
  photo.tags.insert(tag);
}

fn has_tag(photo: &Photo, tag: Str) -> Bool {
  return photo.tags.contains(&tag);
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let photo = Photo{ id: 1, tags: Set[Str].new() };
  add_tag(&mut photo, "sunset");
  add_tag(&mut photo, "beach");

  io.println(has_tag(&photo, "sunset"));  // true
  io.println(has_tag(&photo, "urban"));   // false
  return 0;
}
```

### Comparing with Vec

A Vec would let you add "sunset" twice, and checking membership requires a loop. A Set guarantees uniqueness AND gives you O(1) membership checks with `.contains()` — no loop needed.

